# Defence in depth. NOT the authority.
#
# The authority on what an evidence receipt describes is tools/hash-policy.mjs:
# text is LF-normalised before it is hashed or counted, binary is passed through
# untouched, and every receipt declares which rule produced it. That holds no
# matter what a clone puts on disk, which is the property that actually matters.
#
# This file only reduces how often the two diverge in the first place. It cannot
# replace the policy: it has no effect on a file a TOOL writes with the other
# ending, and none at all on a tree checked out before it existed. Both of those
# happened here — the working tree that exposed the defect had 3 of 94 narratives
# in CRLF and 91 in LF, with freshly merged artifacts CRLF and older ones LF.
#
# So: keep both. If they ever disagree, the policy is right.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary must never be touched by eol conversion. These are also the files that
# reach tools/release-audit.mjs through the same receipt path as data/*.json,
# where a normalising hash would corrupt the identity being pinned.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.wav  binary
*.ogg  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip  binary
*.pdf  binary
